WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:14.227
Idea. Try reading out one zero one one, one zero zero one, quickly, without a slip. It is hard. Programmers write binary in a shorter way called hexadecimal, or hex. Hex is base sixteen.

00:00:14.800 --> 00:00:27.567
Base sixteen needs sixteen digits. Hex uses nought to nine, then letters for ten to fifteen. A is ten, B is eleven, C is twelve, D is thirteen, E is fourteen and F is fifteen.

00:00:28.150 --> 00:00:39.448
Four bits, a nibble, can make exactly sixteen patterns, from nought to fifteen. So one hex digit stands for exactly one nibble, and a byte is always two hex digits.

00:00:40.550 --> 00:00:50.033
Method. To convert a byte to hex, split it into two nibbles. Give each nibble the headers eight, four, two, one, and add the values above the ones.

00:00:50.600 --> 00:00:59.568
If a nibble is worth ten to fifteen, swap it for its letter. Then write the two digits in the same order as the nibbles, left nibble first.

00:01:00.700 --> 00:01:14.926
Worked example. Here is a real question from Paper 1 in June 2025. Convert the binary pattern one zero one one, one zero zero one into hexadecimal. It is worth two marks.

00:01:15.500 --> 00:01:22.912
Split the byte into two nibbles. The left nibble is one zero one one. The right nibble is one zero zero one.

00:01:23.500 --> 00:01:28.524
The left nibble is eight plus two plus one, which is eleven. Eleven is B.

00:01:29.100 --> 00:01:43.056
The right nibble is eight plus one, which is nine. Nine stays as nine. Keep the order: B then nine. The answer is B nine. This explanation is by olevels.com, not an official mark scheme.

00:01:44.200 --> 00:01:53.094
Common mistake. A common mistake is writing eleven instead of B. Eleven nine would be three hex digits, but a byte is only two.

00:01:53.650 --> 00:02:04.754
Another mistake is thinking hex saves memory or is faster for the computer. It does neither. The computer still stores binary. Hex is only easier for humans to read.

00:02:05.900 --> 00:02:15.633
Your turn. Now you try. Convert the binary pattern zero one one one, one one one zero into hexadecimal. Pause here and work it out.

00:02:16.750 --> 00:02:31.056
Answer. The left nibble, zero one one one, is four plus two plus one, which is seven. The right nibble, one one one zero, is eight plus four plus two, which is fourteen, and fourteen is E. The answer is seven E.

00:02:31.650 --> 00:02:43.097
Remember the method: split into nibbles, use eight, four, two, one on each, swap ten to fifteen for A to F, and keep the order. Now try the practice questions for this type.
